# XSH aio_read()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The control blocks and buffers are static and stay valid until the
# request has completed. Every wait is bounded. EBADF and EINVAL may be
# found by the call or reported as the request's error status (the page
# allows both), so each test accepts both. A request that cannot complete
# is made with a read of an empty pipe.

assertion  XSH.aio_read.001
anchor     functions/aio_read.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/aio_read.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       With the descriptor's own offset elsewhere, aio_read() of 4
           bytes at aio_offset 6 returns 0, the request completes with error
           status 0 and return status 4, and the buffer holds the bytes at
           offset 6.
test       t/001-offset.c

assertion  XSH.aio_read.002
anchor     functions/aio_read.html#ERRORS
anchor     functions/aio_read.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       Reading 8 bytes at 4 bytes before the end of a regular file
           transfers the 4 bytes that remain (return status 4); at the end
           and beyond it the return status is 0 and the buffer is left as it
           was.
note       The return status is one of the values read() returns, and
           read() of a regular file at or past the end gives 0.
test       t/002-eof.c

assertion  XSH.aio_read.003
anchor     functions/aio_read.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
anchor     functions/aio_read.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With aio_nbytes 0 the call either fails with EINVAL or the
           request completes with error status 0, return status 0 and the
           buffer unchanged; the other outcome of an EINVAL error status
           comes with return status -1.
note       The page calls an invalid aio_nbytes EINVAL but does not say
           whether zero is invalid (Q-154), so both outcomes are
           accepted.
test       t/003-zero.c

assertion  XSH.aio_read.004
anchor     functions/aio_read.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       aio_lio_opcode is ignored: with LIO_WRITE or LIO_NOP in it, a
           request submitted by aio_read() on a read-only descriptor still
           reads, and the file is not changed.
test       t/004-opcode.c

assertion  XSH.aio_read.005
anchor     functions/aio_read.html#ERRORS/EBADF
class      required
since      2001
says       A descriptor of -1, a closed descriptor and a write-only
           descriptor are each rejected with EBADF by the call, or the
           request has error status EBADF and return status -1.
test       t/005-ebadf.c

assertion  XSH.aio_read.006
anchor     functions/aio_read.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
class      required
since      2001
says       A negative aio_offset on a regular file is rejected with EINVAL
           by the call, or the request has error status EINVAL and return
           status -1.
test       t/006-einval-offset.c

assertion  XSH.aio_read.007
anchor     functions/aio_read.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.8.2
class      disputed
when       PIO
since      2001
says       A negative aio_reqprio is rejected with EINVAL by the call, or
           the request has error status EINVAL and return status -1.
note       disputed (Q-153): the EINVAL entry for an invalid priority is
           unconditional on the file, but the priority applies only to
           files for which the implementation supports prioritization, and
           which those are is implementation-defined. Read strictly here
           (a regular file is used).
test       t/007-reqprio.c

assertion  XSH.aio_read.008
anchor     functions/aio_read.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.4.2
class      required
since      2001
says       With SIGEV_SIGNAL, SIGRTMIN with SA_SIGINFO set, and a
           sigev_value pointer, a signal SIGRTMIN carrying that pointer in
           si_value is delivered, and the request is complete (error status
           0, return status 4, data read) when it is.
test       t/008-sigev-signal.c

assertion  XSH.aio_read.009
anchor     functions/aio_read.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       aio_read() of an empty pipe returns 0 although no data can be
           delivered; the request is then in progress (EINPROGRESS) until a
           byte is written, and then completes with error status 0, return
           status 1 and that byte.
test       t/009-pending.c
